Benchmarking CIVS
=================

    tests/bench/run-bench

This measures how quickly CIVS can record votes, by building a throwaway
installation, serving it over real HTTP, and voting in it from concurrent
clients making real requests. Nothing outside tests/bench/work is touched,
no mail is sent, and no existing poll data is read or written.

It is a benchmark, not a test: it reports numbers rather than passing or
failing, and the numbers belong to the machine, the filesystem and the
perl they were taken on. Compare them against other numbers taken the same
way, and against nothing else.


An open workload
----------------

Voters arrive at a fixed rate, and that rate owes nothing to how the
server is coping. The generator waits

    -k * ln(U),   U drawn from [0,1)

between issuing one request and issuing the next -- a Poisson process of
rate 1/k -- and issues each one whether or not the requests before it have
come back.

This is the open system of Schroeder, Wierman and Harchol-Balter, "Open
Versus Closed: A Cautionary Tale" (NSDI '06). The distinction matters more
than it sounds. A closed generator -- some fixed number of simulated
clients, each sending again only once its previous request has returned --
throttles itself exactly when the server slows down. Load is capped at the
number of clients however slow things get, the queue can never grow, and
so a closed generator cannot show a server falling over: it reports a
comfortable-looking response time at every load, right up to loads the
server could never really have carried. An open generator can show it, and
that is what this one is for.

The consequence is that an overloaded run has requests piling up with
nothing to stop them. The generator calls the run off when too many are
outstanding at once (--max-inflight) rather than letting the machine
thrash, and reports that as the poll having fallen over, which it is.

run-bench raises the rate from run to run until the poll can no longer
keep up. What comes out is not a single number but the curve of response
time against offered load, and the rate at which it turns upwards.


What is measured
----------------

Response time is counted from the moment a voter meant to arrive, not from
the moment the generator got round to the socket, so any delay of the
generator's own is charged to the measurement rather than quietly
forgiven. Each run reports how late it issued its arrivals; if that figure
is not small next to the response times, the generator was the bottleneck
and the run is worthless.

A run is judged to have fallen over if any of these holds:

  - too many requests were outstanding at once, so the run was called off;
  - fewer votes were recorded than arrived, by more than the work a server
    that was keeping up would have had in hand anyway;
  - requests failed, or never finished.

There is deliberately no separate test of whether the backlog grew.
Nothing leaves the system except by completing, so the votes that arrived
during the window and were not recorded during it are exactly the ones
still in the queue when it closed: the shortfall in throughput and the
growth of the backlog are one number seen from either end. To judge a run
on both would be to convict it on a single piece of evidence counted
twice -- and since a queue near capacity wanders up and down a good deal,
whichever of the two was read more loosely would decide every case.

What that number is measured against is the work a server keeping up
would have had in hand at any moment: at a rate of lambda with responses
taking R there are always about lambda*R requests being worked on, which
the run measures for itself as the occupancy when the window opened. That
much is allowed, plus a twentieth of the window's arrivals for the
wandering of a random arrival process, and a few requests so that a short
run is not condemned by two or three of them. Beyond that, the server is
falling behind.

A queue that rose through a window the server nonetheless kept up with is
not a failure, but it is where the failure will be, so it is reported:

    VERDICT: kept up, but the backlog rose from 5.4 to 14.9 outstanding:
             the next rate may be too much

The second test needs enough arrivals to mean anything, since a short run
is mostly luck. Below thirty measured arrivals only outright failure is
looked for, and the run says as much rather than pronouncing on a rate it
cannot see.

That rule is the whole difference between a benchmark that finds where a
poll gives way and one that stops at the first rate to make the queue
wobble -- and when a rate near the limit is called a failure, the
temptation is to loosen a threshold until it is not. So runs already
taken are kept in load.js alongside what they plainly were, and

    node tests/bench/load.js --check

puts them back through the judgement. It needs no server and votes in
nothing. Change the rule and it will say which of those runs the change
has altered its mind about.

A 200 that is not the page that was wanted -- a poll refusing the ballot,
an error page -- counts as a failure. Otherwise a run in which every vote
was turned away would look wonderfully fast.

Each rate gets a poll of its own by default, so that the rates can be
compared with each other. Pass --reuse-poll to vote in one poll
throughout: what CIVS costs to vote in grows with the number of votes
already cast, since every request loads and rewrites the whole list of
voter keys, so a run against a poll that is filling up measures something
real but different.


The pieces
----------

  run-bench       builds the installation, starts the server, walks the
                  arrival rate upwards, and prints the table at the end
  cgi-server.pl   a small CGI web server: enough of CGI/1.1 to run the
                  CIVS scripts over a socket, and no more
  load.js         the load generator: one arrival rate per run
  make-poll.pl    creates and starts the poll to vote in, by running the
                  installed CGI scripts as a supervisor would

node is needed for the generator, as it is for the browser-side tests.

A high rate holds many sockets open at once on both sides of the loopback
interface, so run-bench raises its own descriptor limit before it starts.
Where the system will not allow that, the server begins refusing
connections, which reads as the poll having fallen over when it is really
the machine that has.

The server forks a process and starts a perl interpreter for every
request, as mod_cgi does, and serves a fixed number of requests at once
(--workers, 8 by default). That worker count and the listen backlog are
the server's shape, not the workload's: they are the c and the queue of an
M/G/c station. Raising them lets more of the machine be used and lets more
requests wait; neither makes a vote take less time to record.

CIVS reads a voter's address from the forwarding headers a reverse proxy
would set, so the generator sets X-Real-IP and one machine can stand in
for as many voters as the run needs. Without that, every ballot would come
from 127.0.0.1 and the second one would be turned away as a repeat vote.
Ballots are posted as multipart/form-data, which is what the ballot form
itself asks browsers to send.


Reading the output
------------------

    === 2: 80 votes/s ===
      offered 80.00 votes/s  (mean wait k = 12.5 ms)
      arrivals 796 measured (79.60/s), 1030 issued in all
      completed 796 ok, 0 failed, 0 outstanding at the end
      throughput 79.30 votes/s recorded during the window
      response  mean 81 ms   median 77   p90 96   p99 132   max 141 ms
      in system mean 6.5, most 16  (first third 6.2, last third 7.0)
      generator issued arrivals a median of 0.9 ms late, at worst 3 ms
      VERDICT: kept up

"in system" is the number of votes being worked on at once, sampled ten
times a second. Its first and last thirds are given because a server past
its capacity does not slow down gracefully under an open workload: the
number in the system climbs for as long as the run lasts. Little's law
holds here, so mean in system should be about the arrival rate times the
mean response time -- 80 x 0.081 is 6.5, which is what it says -- and a
run where it is not is a run to distrust.

The last run of a ramp is the interesting one:

    === 3: 160 votes/s ===
      completed 1200 ok, 347 failed, 0 outstanding at the end
      throughput 119.70 votes/s recorded during the window
      response  mean 2561 ms   median 1559   p90 5158   p99 13229
      in system mean 258.0, most 357  (first third 202.8, last third 306.2)
      180 x connection: write EPIPE
      VERDICT: fell over -- recorded 119.7 of the 154.7 votes/s arriving,
               leaving 350 unfinished when the window closed; 347 of 1547
               requests failed

Response time has gone from 81 ms to two and a half seconds for a rate
twice as high, which is the shape of every queue past its capacity. Note
what failure looks like from outside: connections refused and reset, as
the queue of connections waiting to be accepted overflows. That is what an
overloaded server does, but it is the server's shape failing rather than
anything about counting votes -- the accept queue on most systems is
clamped well below what --backlog asks for. Read those as the symptom of
the rate being too high, not as a fault of their own.

The table at the end gathers the runs together:

       offered achieved      ok  failed    mean     p50     p90     p99  verdict
         40.00    40.90     415       0      71      70      76      84  kept-up
         80.00    79.30     796       0      81      77      96     132  kept-up
        160.00   119.70    1200     347    2561    1559    5158   13229  fell-over

      it kept up at 80 votes/s and fell over at 160
      the most it recorded in any run was 119.7 votes/s


Options
-------

    --start R      first arrival rate, votes/second (default 1)
    --step F       multiply the rate by this after each run (default 1.5)
    --max R        stop at this rate even if the poll is still keeping up
    --steps N      run at no more than this many rates (default 12)
    --duration S   seconds measured at each rate (default 20)
    --warmup S     seconds run before measuring, at each rate (default 3)
    --choices N    choices in the poll (default 5)
    --questions N  questions in the poll (default 1)
    --mode M       vote:    cast a ballot, one POST per question
                   ballot:  fetch the ballot form and nothing else
                   session: fetch the form, then cast the ballot
    --workers N    requests the server will serve at once (default 8)
    --reuse-poll   vote in one poll throughout
    --settings F   settings file to install from (default config/sample)
    -k             keep the installation, and the server log, afterwards

A run of a dozen rates at the default duration takes a few minutes. For a
quick look:

    tests/bench/run-bench --start 5 --step 1.7 --steps 6 --duration 10

The default is deliberately gentle at the start: the first rates are meant
to be easy, so that the response time at no load is on the table to
compare the rest against.
